**Reused evidence**
- `E0001` and `E0002` are reused as authority / formal-power context only; they establish Abdel Fattah el-Sisi’s presidency and executive authority, not economic performance by themselves.
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["5B.1"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.10"]}

**New evidence**
- IMF staff said Egypt’s authorities had already expanded targeted social protection and implemented exchange-rate flexibility, and that continued exchange-rate flexibility plus prudent fiscal and monetary policies were needed to preserve macroeconomic stability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"International Monetary Fund Statement on Egypt","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-23","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/23/pr2285-egypt-international-monetary-fund-statement-on-egypt","claim":"IMF staff said the authorities had recently expanded targeted social protection and implemented exchange-rate flexibility, and that continued exchange-rate flexibility plus prudent fiscal and monetary policies were needed to preserve macroeconomic stability.","locator":"paragraph with the IMF mission chief's statement; lines 244-245","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF statement issued contemporaneously with Egypt's reform discussions and directly describing the authorities' actions and needed policies.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"directly within 2022 and contemporaneous to the March shock-response period","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to el-Sisi as president overseeing the executive branch, but implementation also depended on the CBE and finance ministry and was driven by the Ukraine shock.","contrary_evidence":["The statement describes early steps and policy needs, not proven durable success.","It does not itself show distributional outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The World Bank said Egypt responded to the Ukraine shock and tightening global conditions with a 16 percent exchange-rate depreciation, a policy-rate increase, and a 130 billion Egyptian pound mitigation package, while warning that growth would slow and poverty could rise.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt's Economic Update - April 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/egypt/publication/economic-update-april-2022","claim":"The World Bank said Egypt allowed the exchange rate to depreciate by about 16 percent and raised policy rates by 100 basis points, while the government introduced a 130 billion Egyptian pound mitigation package to offset price rises.","locator":"Recent Developments section in the April 2022 update","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country update is a primary institutional assessment; the page summary directly reports the policy moves and their macroeconomic context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"fully within 2022, describing the immediate March 2022 policy response","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to el-Sisi through the executive branch and the CBE, but the actions are also responses to external shocks and central-bank decisions.","contrary_evidence":["The same update stresses underlying structural weaknesses and slower growth ahead.","It does not prove that the policy mix was sufficient or well executed."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Carnegie described Egypt’s military as spearheading the state-led investment strategy under Sisi and controlling a vast economic portfolio, with privileged access to land, procurement, and other advantages.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Retain, Restructure, or Divest? Policy Options for Egypt's Military Economy","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-01-31","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2022/01/retain-restructure-or-divest-policy-options-for-egypts-military-economy?lang=en","claim":"Carnegie said the Egyptian military had spearheaded the state-led investment strategy Sisi pursued and controlled a vast economic portfolio, including public works, land access, and multiple civilian sectors.","locator":"Introduction, lines 52-56 and 65-67","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Serious policy analysis with detailed sectoral description and explicit attribution to Sisi's state-led strategy, though it remains interpretive rather than official data.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"describes the inherited structure and active 2022 state-led model","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Sisi as the central executive sponsor of the state-led investment model, though the military and other agencies executed much of it.","contrary_evidence":["The paper also notes some practical benefits from military-managed infrastructure and housing.","It is an analytical assessment, not an audited balance sheet."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.5","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- Carnegie said the IMF and World Bank had identified an uneven playing field around Egyptian military companies and argued for competitive neutrality, unified governance, and stronger anti-monopoly rules.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Throwing Down the Gauntlet: What the IMF Can Do About Egypt's Military Companies","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2022/04/throwing-down-the-gauntlet-what-the-imf-can-do-about-egypts-military-companies?center=middle-east","claim":"Carnegie said the IMF had recognized Egyptian military companies as part of the SOE landscape and that many benefited from an uneven playing field, calling for competitive neutrality and stronger anti-monopoly rules.","locator":"lines 94, 141, 175, 220-221, 236","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed policy paper drawing on World Bank and IMF material; the claim is well-supported but still an expert synthesis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"within 2022 and directly about the policy environment Sisi faced","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi-era political economy because it describes the state and military privileges operating under his presidency, though the paper itself is analytical.","contrary_evidence":["The paper also notes the government had begun discussing private-sector empowerment and state-asset consolidation.","It does not itself prove that reforms were not attempted."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
- Reuters reported that Sisi appointed Hassan Abdalla as caretaker central bank governor in August 2022 after Tarek Amer resigned during the currency crisis; Abdalla had long banking experience and prior liberal-policy support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt's Sisi names Hassan Abdalla as caretaker cbank head","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Egypt-s-Sisi-names-Hassan-Abdalla-as-caretaker-cbank-head-41353858/","claim":"Reuters reported that Sisi appointed Hassan Abdalla as caretaker governor of the central bank after Tarek Amer resigned during the currency crisis, and that Abdalla had senior banking experience and had supported liberal economic policies.","locator":"Reuters text at lines 826-837 and 839-842","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a concrete appointment, with background on the appointee's credentials and policy orientation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as president making the appointment; competence inference is limited to Abdalla's background and market reaction.","contrary_evidence":["The post was caretaker/acting, so it shows a personnel response more than a durable institutional choice.","The source does not show that Abdalla changed policy outcomes by itself."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The IMF staff-level agreement in October 2022 said Egypt’s reform package would safeguard macroeconomic stability, strengthen the social safety net, and support private-sector-led growth and job creation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt: IMF Reaches Staff-Level Agreement on an Extended Fund Facility Arrangement","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/10/26/pr22363-egypt-imf-reaches-staff-level-agreement-on-an-extended-fund-facility-arrangement","claim":"IMF staff and the Egyptian authorities reached a staff-level agreement on reforms to safeguard macroeconomic stability, strengthen the social safety net, and underpin private-sector-led growth and job creation.","locator":"press release summary and mission statement; lines 238-251","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF negotiation document naming the authorities' agreed policy package and implementation steps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Sisi's government and economic team, though still conditional on IMF approval and implementation by executive agencies.","contrary_evidence":["This is a staff-level agreement, not proof of completed implementation.","It reflects IMF conditionality and external pressure as well as domestic choice."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Reuters reported that Egypt’s annual urban inflation reached 18.7 percent in November 2022, the highest since 2017, after the October devaluation and import restrictions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt inflation jumps to five-year high of 18.7% in November","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/egypts-annual-urban-consumer-inflation-accelerates-to-187-in-nov--capmas-2959749","claim":"Reuters reported that Egypt's annual urban consumer inflation rose to 18.7 percent in November 2022, the highest since 2017, after the October currency devaluation and continued import restrictions.","locator":"lines 422-430","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report using CAPMAS data, with a clear causal chain to policy and supply constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Only partially attributable to Sisi because the inflation spike also reflected external shocks, import shortages, and market conditions, but the devaluation and import restrictions were policy choices of his government.","contrary_evidence":["The report itself cites supply shortages and higher production costs, so not all inflation is ruler-caused.","This is an outcome indicator, not a direct policy statement."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The IMF’s December 2022 approval said Egypt’s package would shift to a flexible exchange rate, reduce the state footprint, level the playing field, strengthen governance and transparency, and expand social spending.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Approves 46-month US$3 billion Extended Arrangement for Egypt","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/16/pr22441-egypt-imf-executive-board-approves-46-month-usd3b-extended-arrangement","claim":"The IMF approved Egypt's arrangement and said the package would shift to a flexible exchange rate, reduce the state footprint, level the playing field, strengthen governance and transparency, and expand social spending.","locator":"lines 258-271","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF approval statement describing the agreed policy package and the rationale for it.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Sisi's government as the borrower-side authority, but conditioned by IMF approval and implementation by the CBE, cabinet, and state agencies.","contrary_evidence":["It is a program approval, not proof of implementation success by year-end.","It reflects external conditionality as well as domestic initiative."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The World Bank’s December 2022 monitor said inflation and portfolio outflows were squeezing Egypt’s public finances, buffers had fallen since March, growth would slow, poverty could rise, and education spending remained at 2 percent of GDP.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt Economic Monitor, December 2022: Strengthening Resilience through Fiscal and Education Sectors Reforms","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/egypt/publication/egypt-economic-monitor-fall-2022-strengthening-resilience-through-fiscal-and-education-sectors-reforms","claim":"The World Bank said Egypt faced a spike in inflation and large portfolio outflows, foreign-exchange buffers had fallen since March 2022, growth would slow, poverty could rise, and education spending remained at 2 percent of GDP for FY2022/23.","locator":"lines 39-46","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank country monitor with explicit 2022 macro, poverty, and education-budget discussion.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"within 2022 and describing year-end conditions","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Sisi's government through fiscal and exchange-rate choices, but heavily constrained by the Russia-Ukraine war and global financial tightening.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes that the government announced social mitigation packages and support from the IMF program.","It does not isolate ruler choice from external shocks."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The World Bank’s December 2022 social-protection financing expanded Takaful and Karama, covering 3.69 million households, with 74 percent of beneficiaries being women and 67 percent of cash going to Upper Egypt.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New World Bank Financing Will Expand and Strengthen Egypt's Flagship Social Protection Program","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/23/new-world-bank-financing-will-expand-and-strengthen-egypt-s-flagship-social-protection-program","claim":"The World Bank announced new financing to expand Takaful and Karama, which as of June 2022 covered 3.69 million households; women were 74 percent of beneficiaries and 67 percent of the cash went to Upper Egypt.","locator":"lines 146-154","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with direct program coverage and distribution details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government's social-protection apparatus, though the program was co-financed and shaped with the World Bank.","contrary_evidence":["This shows mitigation and targeting, not a full answer to broad inequality or labor-market weakness.","It does not prove that the poor were adequately protected from inflation."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}
- In a December 2022 World Bank readout, Malpass and Sisi discussed devaluation, Ukraine-war effects, food-price pressure, and the importance of cash transfers, while welcoming the State Ownership Policy and SOE reforms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout from World Bank Group President David Malpass's Meeting with President Abdel Fattah El-Sisi of Egypt","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/statement/2022/12/16/readout-from-world-bank-group-president-david-malpass-s-meeting-with-president-abdel-fattah-el-sisi-of-egypt","claim":"The World Bank readout said Malpass and Sisi discussed the latest currency devaluation, the effects of the Russian invasion of Ukraine, energy and fertilizer shortages, rising food prices, and the importance of well-targeted cash transfers; Malpass also welcomed Egypt's recent State Ownership Policy and SOE reforms.","locator":"lines 24-28","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank readout from a head-of-state meeting, with direct references to policy priorities and macro shocks.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as the president in the meeting, but still framed by external shocks and partner conditionality.","contrary_evidence":["A readout is diplomatic context rather than a detailed implementation audit.","It does not show how much reform was actually delivered by year-end."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Reuters reported on December 30, 2022 that Egypt lifted import curbs and Sisi approved a list of 62 economic activities the state would leave to the private sector to satisfy IMF requirements.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt lifts import curbs, backs bigger private sector role to meet IMF demands","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-12-30","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Egypt-lifts-import-curbs-backs-bigger-private-sector-role-to-meet-IMF-demands-42636074/","claim":"Reuters reported that Egypt lifted a key import restriction and that President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi approved a list of 62 economic activities the state would leave to the private sector to meet IMF conditions.","locator":"lines 847-875","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a concrete policy move and a presidential approval tied to IMF conditions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sisi as the president approving the list and to his government implementing the import-policy reversal; IMF conditionality and the dollar shortage were major external constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The move was reactive and tied to IMF demands rather than an autonomous reform agenda.","The article notes continuing foreign-currency shortages and earlier devaluations."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Reuters/Reuters mirrors, Carnegie, SIS, Ahram, EgyptToday, and later-retrospective World Bank/IMF pages.
- Kept 12 new URLs above and the two reused authority items.
- Dropped duplicates or near-duplicates: repeated IMF program releases, repeated World Bank summaries of the same macro shock, and multiple mirror copies of the same Reuters stories.
- Dropped weaker or redundant official spin: SIS/Ahram copies of the State Ownership Policy were not needed once the Reuters December 30 report and World Bank readout were retained; the core claim was duplicated.
- Dropped out-of-period or too-retrospective pages for final evidence: 2024 US State Department investment-climate material, 2025 IMF country pages, and 2026 World Bank country/PIP pages were checked only as later context, not as target-year evidence.
- Dropped blocked or inaccessible pages where the page itself rejected fetches, such as the CBE bio page; Reuters and MarketScreener copies were used instead when they gave the needed 2022 appointment detail.
- I also considered but did not retain some budget and cabinet-press snippets because the stronger evidence already captured the same policy direction with better attribution and less duplication.

**Remaining gaps**
- Competent-economist staffing is only partially covered. I have a solid appointment example for the central bank, but not a full audited picture of the 2022 economic team or how much autonomy it had.
- Anti-corruption and capture evidence is strong on structure and privileges, but weaker on concrete enforcement actions against entrenched interests in 2022. More searching could verify whether the State Ownership Policy or SOE reforms produced measurable divestments by year-end.
- Distributional effects are better covered on social protection than on wages, consumption, and poverty outcomes. A household survey or CAPMAS/WB poverty update would improve attribution on who benefited or lost in 2022.
- The end-of-year trajectory is clear on inflation, shortages, and reform pressure, but a direct 2023 retrospective on whether the December 2022 reforms actually improved macro conditions would help separate announcement from implementation.
